course: dictionary
lesson: 5540
title: One word, several meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and the context tells you which one the writer means. Legend is a good example. «A legend says three brothers, Kyi, Shchek and Khoryv, and their sister Lybid founded the city» is about an old story from long ago, not a map or a famous player.
explain: Look for clues. A legend is an old story that may be partly true but cannot be proved. In «it is a legend, like Romulus and Remus for Rome», the writer tells you straight away what kind of story it is. In «the exact founding year is not known», the clue is that nobody can prove the story, which is what makes it a legend.
explain: Legend has other meanings too. In «a football legend», it means a very famous person who is admired for what they did. On a map, the legend is the key that explains what the symbols and colours mean. Try each meaning in the sentence and keep the one that makes sense.
fact: A legend is an old, famous story from the past that may be partly true but cannot be proved.
fact: Legends are usually about humans and places, like King Arthur or Robin Hood, while myths are usually about gods.
fact: «A school legend» is often told about "a kid at my cousin's school", and that friend-of-a-friend detail is a clue that it may be made up.
fact: «A football legend» means a very famous person who is admired for what they did.
fact: The legend of a map or chart is its key, which explains what the symbols and colours mean.
fact: «You're a legend!» is a friendly way of saying «you're great».
exercise: «A legend says three brothers, Kyi, Shchek and Khoryv, and their sister Lybid founded the city.» In this sentence, what is a legend? | A kind of ghost | The key to a map | A famous football player | *An old story from the past that cannot be proved
exercise: Mia looks at her map and asks, «What is the legend of this map?» What is she asking about? | A story about pirates | The name of the town | *The key that explains the symbols and colours | The very famous mapmaker
exercise: Ben says, «You're a legend!» What does he mean? | *You're great | You're old and grey | You can't be trusted | You are lost
test: «A school legend is usually told about "a kid at my cousin's school".» What clue shows a school legend may be made up? | The school is very big | *The friend-of-a-friend detail | The kid is called Sam | It is written in a book
test: «Sara is a football legend, admired by everyone who watched her play.» What does legend mean here? | The key of a map | *A very famous person who is admired | A ghost story | An old story from the past
exam: Tom says, «A legend is always a lie.» Is he right? | Yes, every legend is always true | No, it is a map key | Yes, a legend is always a lie | *No, a legend may be partly true but cannot be proved
